{
	title: 'Old tool output is pruned from provider input without breaking the prompt cache',
	spec: `Provider input has a cache-aware, provider-neutral projection which removes old tool output and other heavy payloads without deleting or rewriting conversation truth. History records, signatures, full stored tool output, attachments and the client transcript remain unchanged and recoverable. Pruning is distinct from output capping (bz) and compaction (bc): it keeps the conversation skeleton rather than replacing the conversation with a summary.

CACHE AND SCHEDULING. Routine pruning is deterministic from the active replay history after its latest compact/reset and uses stable completed-turn checkpoints, initially every 8 completed conversational turns. Tool rounds, tool calls, retry attempts, pause/continue records and steering inside an unfinished turn do not individually advance that checkpoint. Completion is determined from durable turn semantics, not from a provider message temporarily ending without tool calls. Initially retain heavy content for at least 4 completed turns; ages are measured from the last checkpoint, not from the live end with an age threshold rounded to a batch size. Between checkpoints, appending a prompt or tool round does not change the already-sent old prefix. At a checkpoint any intentional rewrite is performed as one bounded batch; checkpoints reduce cache invalidation rather than magically making rewritten bytes cache hits. No elapsed-time, sliding age or per-request recapping may continuously move the omission frontier. A retry with unchanged history has identical input. Pruning decisions and marker bytes stay stable across host restart and session reopen, including any pressure decisions; rebuilding must not restore payloads previously omitted and then drop them again. Keep the system prompt and tool definitions independent of pruning and free of changing counts or live pruning notices.

EXCEPTIONS. A genuinely cold cache or an explicit compact/reset/model-context rebuild is an opportunity to prune without wasting a reusable prefix, but a provider switch, account rotation or a single cache miss is not proof that every future request is cold. Normal requests must not guess a cold cache from lack of a local flag. Under real context pressure a deliberate extra pruning boundary is allowed to avoid overflowing the window, initially at the smaller of 180,000 estimated tokens and 75% of the model context window; measure system/tools overhead too. Change input only if it actually shrinks. This exceptional prefix rewrite is observable and remains stable afterwards, not repeated each round merely because the estimate is still high. Protect the newest assistant tool-call group and all its newly returned results/images until the model has had a successful request consuming them; failed requests and retries do not prove consumption. A resumed or paused turn retains its pending/unconsumed results. Preserve signed thinking and any provider-required contiguous thinking/tool sequence while it is needed for valid continuation. If protected content alone is too large, use the existing prompt-too-long/compaction/error path rather than silently losing the work the model has not read.

WHAT STAYS. Preserve user instructions and text, assistant text, compact summaries, timestamps and meta notes; preserve tool names, call IDs, result pairing/order and error status. Keep ordinary tool arguments verbatim: paths, commands, IDs and keys explain what a call did, and valid-looking empty input objects teach the model to make argument-less calls. Only oversized argument values (initially strings over 1,000 characters) may be elided individually, never replaced with an empty object. Old tool-result text and associated tool images may become small omission markers. Old user images may be omitted only under the same protection/recovery rules; user text and attachments expanded as instructions are not silently discarded. This task does not require age-pruning thinking: if implemented, it obeys the same frozen checkpoints (initial age 10 turns), exact signature/continuation constraints and recovery rules, not an independent sliding cutoff. Tool-call/result repair and provider schema validation still apply after pruning; private projection metadata must never leak into API block fields.

RECOVERY AND SAFETY. Each omission tells the model that content was omitted, not that the tool returned the marker as its real output, and names an existing immutable recovery reference with a short read_blob instruction, for example [pruned tool output; read_blob id=sessionId#35]. Use Hal durable session#record numbers for original calls/results, and session/blob IDs for images or whole capped output. A capped result which already names a full-output blob keeps that blob recoverable in its pruned marker: recovering only its preview is not equivalent to recovering its retained full output. read_blob reads original history records or blobs, never the pruned projection; text is paged and bounded, images return as images, references are validated, and missing references are explicit errors. A recovered read_blob result is fresh tool output and receives the same newest-unconsumed protection. Do not require the model to re-run tools, re-read live files or guess values to recover an immutable earlier result. Model-copied generated pruning markers anywhere in a tool input are rejected before dispatch with a recoverable error asking it to read the named record/blob and retry with the actual value; placeholders must never execute as commands or be written as file contents. Match the reserved generated marker form, not arbitrary prose containing the word pruned.

INTEGRATION AND ACCEPTANCE. Build the projection on the host history-to-provider path shared by all real providers (history.messages and turns.runTurn), not in clients or solely in one provider mapper; compact, edited-prompt and reset boundaries retain their existing semantics. Status usage still comes from each provider round (k5) and honestly reflects its post-pruning input. A small behavioral regression exercises a long tool-heavy history over multiple checkpoints: all earlier serialized provider-content bytes remain unchanged between allowed boundaries, including many tool rounds and unchanged-history retries; at a boundary old eligible outputs shrink while fresh results and call arguments remain. Another focused recovery/safety case checks read_blob retrieves original omitted/capped content and a copied marker cannot reach a mutating tool. Check restart/rebuild identity and tool-pairing/thinking validity using existing replay/provider coverage rather than source-text tests. Evaluate cache-read, cache-write and uncached-input usage separately on a representative run: fewer context tokens is not proof of lower quota use, and an unexpected cache-write increase is a regression to investigate. Artifacts old-api-messages.ts, old-api-messages.test.ts, old-agent-loop.ts and old-read_blob.ts are historical reference only, not files to lift into src; implement against Hal neutral blocks and numbered records.

DURABLE PROJECTION DETAILS. Provider-only omission and successful-consumption decisions live in a per-session projection.ason sidecar, atomically saved through liveFiles before reuse. Each eight-completion checkpoint gets at most one successful pressure rewrite, logged in diagnostics; persistent pressure after that follows the existing prompt-too-long/compaction path. Thinking is retained verbatim. read_blob also accepts a validated 1-based charOffset for bounded, lossless character paging of long lines and JSON that ordinary line paging clips; single tool-result records recover their original output as text, and associated images as images. Copied reserved markers in input keys as well as values are rejected.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'History provenance: inspected the last 500 old-Hal commits and current tracked source at 6a8ce5280fccae8d4a24c81825eed7dc512b3cb5. Reference artifacts are exact committed files from that revision: src/server/session/api-messages.ts and its tests, src/server/runtime/agent-loop.ts, and src/server/tools/read_blob.ts. They use old HistoryEntry/blob/provider shapes; do not lift them. Hal rebuilds input in src/host/history.ts:history.messages via src/common/replay.ts:replay.toMessages, called afresh each provider round by src/host/turns.ts. Its read_blob already accepts durable session#record IDs and paged blobs, so recovery does not require porting the old WeakMap.',
		'Cache failure chronology: 91a2bd2612698ac0f2845c19dcdb5be8d4e9a24b (2026-04-10) introduced 8-turn checkpoint batching with frozenAge = age - (completedTurns % batch). 7b873f34b221ca252a66ffb16f6207c948c10fb2, minutes later, removed the frozen age and used pastBatchThreshold on live age while claiming to restore batching. That only rounded a threshold; each subsequent turn pruned another old turn. fbdaf208f612bc6b949579e47cca33b3ea8605a3 (2026-09-25) restored checkpoint-relative ages and added the multi-checkpoint prefix-invariance test: old history changes at turns 4, 8, 12 for a batch of 4, not every turn. Its commit reports about 200K cache tokens rewritten on most user prompts in long tool-heavy Opus sessions.',
		'Failure story and evidence limit: the planning request recalls a whole quota exhausted in roughly 20 minutes through many tool calls. The inspected commit history supports the repeated-cache-write mechanism, but does not establish that exact duration or a causal count of re-reading tools; treat those as reported symptoms, not measured facts. 2b8dcac split uncached input, cacheRead and cacheCreation because previously lumped input hid quota consumption; its old cost weights were 10% cache reads versus 125% cache writes. Prefix invalidation, not just context size, is the documented expensive failure. 1f2bff9 later calibrated a cold-cache warning from one incident and 8deab52 removed it for bad calibration: do not bake a universal quota-percent-per-token conversion into this task.',
		'Pressure-mode references: 5cd718f7b71788285bdd1e47456c4df52ba666a3 introduced pruning already-consumed heavy payloads at 180k estimated tokens while protecting the newest unconsumed batch; current agent-loop also bounds the trigger by 75% of context. 1d17501dea8c7884787236a39332f41b05dedb07 reports aggregate cache-write share roughly 7.4% before versus 7.1% after (historical 7-24%) and a directional 37% lower cost/call with changed traffic mix. This aggregate was not proof of prefix stability: the checkpoint regression was fixed later in fbdaf20. The specification deliberately requires stable replay and pressure decisions, not blindly copying per-iteration pressure pruning.',
		'Model and data-safety failures: 8fe20b37372fde7b60c7d86be4488b26496fbe4c found 82% of visible old calls had {} input, which the model imitated as argument-less bash/eval calls. Arguments represented only about 0.5% of savings (289KB of 55MB); keeping normal arguments and naming blobs in markers fixed that feedback loop. 876ab11 then rejected recursively model-copied pruning markers before tool dispatch after a marker was written as literal file content. Recoverability and marker rejection are required behavior, not cosmetic wording.',
		'Prerequisites deliberately exclude 8a: that done task concerns web login token-cache pruning, not provider prompt pruning. k5 supplies per-round context/usage reporting, 7 and ne supply durable history and tool-round semantics, bz supplies whole-output recovery, bc supplies existing context boundaries, and 29 supplies stable system-prompt construction. Routine checkpoint batching still permits a deliberate cache rebuild at each checkpoint; cold-cache-only scheduling is not a historical guarantee. The stronger Hal requirements around failed-request consumption, durable pressure decisions and full capped-output recovery prevent foreseeable regressions not covered by the old code.',
		'Implemented shared history projection with a durable liveFiles sidecar, not new history record types. Completion means completed turn_end, never provider round ends. Pressure can rewrite at most once per eight-completion checkpoint; no cold-cache inference or thinking pruning. Omitted records remain recoverable in original history/blobs. replay.current is not idempotent for edited prompts: compute ages from current history but project original records, letting replay apply replacement semantics exactly once.',
		'Regression uses 26 completed turns with 12 signed-thinking/tool rounds each, serialized real Anthropic messages before the mutable newest cache checkpoint, retry identity, rebuild identity, frozen arguments and shrink at checkpoints. Actual live-provider cache-read/write/uncached telemetry was not evaluated in this offline implementation; that acceptance requires a representative authenticated run and must not be inferred from estimated token savings.',
		'Both the normal multi-round turns.runTurn path and direct history.turn calls include the selected model context window plus system/tool overhead in pressure budgeting. Final offline suite had all behavior tests passing; startup wall-clock budget was load-sensitive (157 ms versus 150 ms even in an isolated rerun), with no startup code changed.',
		"Invariant: one image never creates context pressure. Pressure estimated images at file bytes/3 (856 KB PNG = 285k tokens), so a first-message screenshot was pruned right after its first round and the model saw only a read_blob marker. Providers bill downscaled pixels; estimate a flat pruning.imageTokens (1,600, Anthropic's cap) per image.",
		'Measured with count_tokens (2026-10-08): Anthropic image tokens ~ width*height/750 after downscaling. A 3104x1974 screenshot cost 4,733 tokens on Opus 5.5 and 1,572 on Sonnet 4.5; Opus 5.5 caps near 4,800 (2000x2000 gave 4,764; long edge 2576 px), Sonnet 4.5 near 1,600. File bytes do not matter. pruning.imageTokens is 4,800, the largest measured cap.'
	],
	needs: ['7', 'bz', 'ne', 'bc', 'k5', '29']
}
